[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163180 :tutorial/content "Let's remind ourselves how to estimate $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ from sample data.\n\nSuppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 3$ | $8$ |\n| $3\\leq y_i < 5$ | $12$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 7$ | $11$ |\n| $7\\leq y_i < 9$ | $9$ |\n\nSuppose we wish to test the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ $Y\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ $Y\\not\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n\nWe're not given the mean $\\mu$ nor the variance $\\sigma^2$, so we have to estimate them from the sample data.\n\n- For the mean, we have the following estimate:\n$\\widehat{μ}|= \\frac{weighted sum of the midpoints}{total number of observations}; = \\frac{1}{N}∑_(i)O_{i} \\cdot y_{i}^{mid}$\n- For the variance, we have\n$\\widehat{\\sigma}^2 = \\dfrac{1}{N-1}\\sum_i O_i \\cdot (y_i^{\\textrm{mid}} - \\widehat{\\mu})^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\mu}$ is our estimate for the mean $\\mu$, $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ is our estimate for the variance $\\sigma^2$,$N$ is the total number of observations, $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ is the midpoint of an observational class $(a_i, b_i)$.\n\nLet's compute estimates for the mean and variance in this case:\n\nThe total number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 8+12+11+9= 40\n$$\n\nNext, we find the midpoint of each observational class.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ | $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 3$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $3\\leq y_i < 5$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 7$ | $11$ | $6$ |\n| $7\\leq y_i < 9$ | $9$ | $8$ |\n\nOur estimate $\\widehat{\\mu}$ for the mean $\\mu$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\widehat{μ} &= \\frac{1}{N}∑O_{i} \\cdot y_{i}^{mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{40}(8 \\cdot 2 + 12 \\cdot 4 + 11 \\cdot 6 + 9 \\cdot 8) \\\\\n&= 5.05\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, our estimate $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ for the variance $\\sigma^2$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\hat{\\sigma }}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{N - 1}} \\sum  {O}_{i} \\cdot  ( {y}_{i}^{\\text{mid}} - \\hat{\\mu } {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{40 - 1}} ( 8 \\cdot  ( 2 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 12 \\cdot  ( 4 - 5.05 {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\phantom{=} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} + 11 \\cdot  ( 6 - 5.05 {)}^{2} + 9 \\cdot  ( 8 - 5.05 {)}^{2} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.51025 \\ldots  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}4.5}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWhen conducting a chi-square test, we lose a degree of freedom for every estimated parameter:\n\n- If *only one* parameter must be estimated, then we have two constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 2$.\n- If *both* parameters must be estimated, then we have three constraints in total, and\n$\\nu = \\textrm{number of categories} - 3$.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c73b244b-9218-508e-9036-0cee701a30b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10675 :tutorial/title "Estimating the Mean and Variance"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163191 :tutorial/content "Suppose $X$ is a random variable with support\n\n$$\nS=\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n$$\n\nA random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observation $(x_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $12$ | $32$ | $44$ | $12$ |\n\nSuppose we wish to conduct a chi-square goodness-of-fit test with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The sample data fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The sample data does not fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n\nNotice we're not given the success probability $p$. Therefore, we must estimate it from the sample data, which we can do as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p} &= \\frac{total number of successes}{total number of trials} \\\\\n&= (∑(x_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(n \\cdot N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\hat p$ is our estimate of the success probability $p$,\n- $N$ is the number of observations,\n- $n$ is the number of trials in each binomial sequence.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- The total number of observations is given by\n$N=12+32+44+ 12= 100$.\n- The number of trials in each binomial sequence is\n$n=3$.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p} &= (0 \\cdot 12 + 1 \\cdot 32 + 2 \\cdot 44 + 3 \\cdot 12)/(3 \\cdot (100)) \\\\\n&= 0.52\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $B(3,0.52)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2edfd9a2-dab3-5216-b8d4-9e29d3455ff3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10831 :tutorial/title "Estimating a Binomial Probability"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163194 :tutorial/content "Suppose $Y$ is a random variable with support\n\n$$\nS=\\{0,1,2,3, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nA random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observation $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $10$ | $15$ | $22$ | $13$ | $0$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The sample data fit a Poisson distribution $\\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The sample data does not fit a Poisson distribution $\\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n\nNotice we're not given the rate parameter $\\lambda$. Therefore, we must estimate it from the sample data, which we can do as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{λ} &= \\frac{total number of events}{total number of observations} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, and $N$ is the number of observations.\n\nIn our case, the total number of observations is given by\n\n$$\nN= 10+15+22+13 = 60\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{λ} &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 10 + 1 \\cdot 15 + 2 \\cdot 22 + 3 \\cdot 13}{60} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.63\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(1.63)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "116831fd-a4a0-56e5-a616-e169119ed1cb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10834 :tutorial/title "Estimating the Rate Parameter"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163347 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/46/4624ca7ee7660803ddc7165252c3725030b13ab255f56dc5d87e23090c2363bf.svg)\nfor $i = 1,\\ldots,n.$ \n\nWe know that if the population sample size $n$ is small compared to the population size $N,$ then the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic. Therefore, we can form an *estimate* for the population proportion $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n} = \\dfrac1 n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i =  \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, for the mean of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\text{E}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot \\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot np \\\\  & =p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot np(1-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is, it has mean $p$ and variance $\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8ecb0abb-cc8a-58b3-b310-fabeeb9c4aca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6480 :tutorial/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168049 :tutorial/content "Finally, consider an $n$th-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\ny^{(n)} + a_{n-1}(x)y^{(n-1)} + \\cdots + a_1(x)y' + a_0(x)y = g(x),\n$$\n\nand let $\\{y_1,\\dots,y_n\\}$ be a set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\nNow, we’ll derive formulas for the method of variation of parameters.\n\nA particular solution can be written as\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x) + \\cdots + u_n(x)y_n(x).\n$$\n\nTo find $u_1,\\dots,u_n$, we impose $n-1$ auxiliary conditions:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^n u_j'(x)y_j^{(k)}(x) = 0,\n\\qquad\nk=0,1,\\dots,n-2,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the final equation\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j=1}^n u_j'(x)y_j^{(n-1)}(x) = g(x).\n$$\n\nTogether, these $n$ equations form a linear system for $u_1'(x),\\dots,u_n'(x)$ of the form\n\n$$\nY \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{g},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n(\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} & ⋯ & {y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'} & ⋯ & {y}_{n}^{'} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {y}_{1}^{(n-2)} & {y}_{2}^{(n-2)} & ⋯ & {y}_{n}^{(n-2)} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{(n-1)} & {y}_{2}^{(n-1)} & ⋯ & {y}_{n}^{(n-1)}\\end{aligned})\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $W(x) = \\det(Y)$ is the Wronskian. And for each $i=1,\\dots,n,$ define:\n\n- $\\widehat{W}_i(x)$ as the determinant obtained by replacing the $i$th column of $Y$ with $\\mathbf{g},$ and\n\n- $W_i(x)$ as the determinant obtained by deleting the $n$th (final) row and the $i$th column from $Y.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\mathbf{g}$ has zeroes in all components except for the final one (which is equal to $g(x)$), by the properties of determinants we can expand $\\widehat{W}_i(x)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\widehat{W}}_{i}(x) & =(-1)^{n+i}g(x){W}_{i}(x) \\\\  & =(-1)^{n-i}g(x){W}_{i}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we used that $(-1)^{n+i} = (-1)^{n-i}$ because the exponents differ by an even number, $2i.$\n\nNext, by *Cramer's rule*, we get\n\n$$\nu'_i = \\dfrac{\\widehat{W}_i(x)}{W(x)} = (-1)^{n-i} \\frac{g(x)\\,W_i(x)}{W(x)},\n\\qquad\ni=1,\\dots,n,\n$$\n\nand integrating both sides (setting the constant of integration to zero), we obtain\n\n$$\nu_i(x) = (-1)^{n-i}\\int \\frac{g(x)\\,W_i(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\n\\qquad\ni=1,\\dots,n.\n$$\n\nFinally, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=u_1y_1(x)+\\cdots+u_ny_n(x)+y_p(x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ce54cdf-5541-5e4e-964c-883412edcb03" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14794 :tutorial/title "Variation of Parameters for Nth-Order ODEs"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173114 :tutorial/content "Given a contingency table with $n$ levels for the row factor $A_1, A_2, \\ldots A_n$ and $m$ levels for the column factor $B_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_m$, under the hypothesis that the factors are independent, we have that the probability of an individual having the value $A_i$ for the row factor and the value $B_j$ for the column factor is equal to the product of the probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(A_i \\cap B_j) = P(A_i) \\cdot P(B_j), \\qquad i=1,2,\\ldots, n \\quad j=1,2,\\ldots, m\n$$\n\nWe can calculate estimates for the probabilities $P(A_i)$ and $P(B_j)$, which we denote as $\\hat p(A_i)$ and $\\hat p(B_j)$, respectively, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p}(A_{i}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelA_{i}}{total number of observations} \\\\\n\\hat{p}(B_{j}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelB_{j}}{total number of observations}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a sample of size $N$, the expected frequency $E_{ij}$ of the event $A_i\\cap B_j$ under the assumption of independence is given by\n\n$$\nE_{ij} = N\\cdot P(A_i\\cap B_j)\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d0c067f4-6026-56e9-a70d-5422ec374049" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10678 :tutorial/title "Calculating Expected Frequencies in the General Case"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173120 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample to find the proportion of voters who intend to vote for a particular candidate in an upcoming election. Let's assume we initially used a sample of size $n_1$ to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nAfter completing the initial survey, it's decided that a new sample must be carried out, and the resulting confidence interval should have the following features:\n\n- The confidence level of the new confidence interval should be $95\\%$ (unchanged).\n- The new confidence interval should be one-half as wide as the original (increased precision).\n\nTo increase the precision while keeping the confidence level fixed, we'll need to *increase* the sample size. But by how much? Let's determine how much larger the second sample size $n_2$ should be compared to the first to satisfy the new conditions.\n\nFirst, recall that, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nGiven a particular margin of error $\\varepsilon$, we can deduce an approximate sample size:\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} = \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of the confidence intervals $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to half the length of $I_1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = 2\\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} &= (z_{α/2}^{2}\\widehat{p}(1 - \\widehat{p}))/(ε_{1}^{2}), \\\\\nn_{2} &= (z_{α/2}^{2}\\widehat{p}(1 - \\widehat{p}))/(ε_{2}^{2}), \\\\\n\\frac{n_{1}}{n_{2}} &= \\frac{ε_{2}^{2}}{ε_{1}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{ε_{2}}{ε_{1}})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n4 n_1 = n_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{larger}}$ then $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "10e10ea8-01a6-5d1c-8993-4d6fefbe2a38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10765 :tutorial/title "Factors Affecting Sample Size"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173126 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to carry out the following hypothesis test:\n\n- $H_0: p_1 = p_2$\n- $H_1: p_1 > p_2$\n\nAs we just saw, the variance of the difference between the two sampling distributions is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2] = \\dfrac{p_1(1-p_1)}{n_1} + \\dfrac{p_2(1-p_2)}{n_2}\n$$\n\nNow, under the null hypothesis, the two proportions are equal. Thus, assuming that $H_0$ is true, we can set\n\n$$\np_1 = p_2 = p\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[\\widehat{p}_{1} - \\widehat{p}_{2}] &= (p(1 - p))/(n_{1}) + (p(1 - p))/(n_{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[\\widehat{p}_{1} - \\widehat{p}_{2}] &= p(1 - p)(\\frac{1}{n_{1}} + \\frac{1}{n_{2}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the two proportions are equal under the null hypothesis, we can use a **pooled estimate** $\\overline{p}$ of the shared proportion $p$ to form a point estimate.\n\n$$\n\\overline{p} = \\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{n_1+n_2} = \\dfrac{n_1\\widehat{\\,p}_1+n_2\\widehat{\\,p}_2}{n_1+n_2}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_1$ and $x_2$ give the number of sample elements from the first and second samples, respectively, with the characteristic.\n\nThus, our estimate for the variance is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2] =\\overline{p}(1-\\overline{p})\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_1} + \\dfrac{1}{n_2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the pooled variance estimate we saw in a previous lesson.\n\nAs a result, we can use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {(\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2) - (p_1-p_2)} {\\sqrt{\\overline{p}(1-\\overline{p}) \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_1}+\\dfrac{1}{n_2} \\right)} \\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- A pooled variance estimate is preferable to an unpooled estimate when the null hypothesis assumes $p_1 = p_2$. It can be shown that the standard error of the pooled estimate is lower than the unpooled one, making our hypothesis test more reliable.\n- On the other hand, suppose we have the following null hypothesis:\n$H_0: p_1 - p_2 = k \\neq 0$.\nIn this case, the variances cannot be assumed equal, and we must use an unpooled estimate for the variance, i.e.,\n$\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}_1 - \\widehat{\\,p}_2] = \\dfrac{p_1(1-p_1)}{n_1} + \\dfrac{p_2(1-p_2)}{n_2}$.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll only consider null hypotheses of the form $p_1 = p_2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "388c4ec8-6df7-5308-9eba-d8575c965a0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10795 :tutorial/title "Pooled vs. Unpooled Estimates of Proportion"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173133 :tutorial/content "We can conduct hypothesis tests to determine whether two variables are independent.\n\nSuppose we want to determine whether the pass rates of students sitting a particular math exam are independent of the school they attend. The district superintendent conducts a random sample of $60$ students who sat the exam from two schools in the district, and they get the following two-way frequency table (also called a **contingency table**) of results.\n\n|  | Pass | Fail |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| School 1 | $18$ | $6$ |\n| School 2 | $24$ | $12$ |\n\nThis is a $2\\times 2$ contingency table because we have two levels for the row factors (School 1 and School 2) and two for the column factors (Pass and Fail).\n\nWe wish to test the following hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:\\:$ The pass rates are independent of the choice of school\n- $H_1:\\:\\:$ The pass rates are *not* independent of the choice of school\n\nThe first step in conducting our hypothesis test is calculating the **expected frequency** for every possible outcome, assuming that the null hypothesis is true.\n\nFirst, we must find the probabilities associated with each row factor, i.e., we must find\n\n$P(\\textrm{Student is from School 1}), \\qquad P(\\textrm{Student is from School 2})$\nand for each column factor\n$P(\\textrm{Student passes}), \\qquad P(\\textrm{Student fails})$.\n\nWe're not given these probabilities. However, they can be estimated from the sample data. We'll use the notation $\\hat p(A)$ to denote our sample estimate of $P(A){:}$\n\nSo, in our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\hat{p}(Student is from School 1) &= \\frac{18 + 6}{60} = 0.4 \\\\\n\\hat{p}(Student is from School 2) &= \\frac{24 + 12}{60} = 0.6 \\\\\n\\hat{p}(Student passes the exam) &= \\frac{18 + 24}{60} = 0.7 \\\\\n\\hat{p}(Student fails the exam) &= \\frac{6 + 12}{60} = 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that if two events $A$ and $B$ are independent, then\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B) = P(A)\\cdot P(B)\n$$\n\nLet's estimate the probability of each combination of values of $A$ and $B$ *under the assumption of independence:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(School 1∩Pass) &= \\hat{p}(Student is from School 1) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Student passes) = 0.28 \\\\\nP(School 1∩Fail) &= \\hat{p}(Student is from School 1) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Student fails) = 0.12 \\\\\nP(School 2∩Pass) &= \\hat{p}(Student is from School 2) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Student passes) = 0.42 \\\\\nP(School 2∩Fail) &= \\hat{p}(Student is from School 2) \\cdot \\hat{p}(Student fails) = 0.18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following table of probabilities:\n\n|  | Pass | Fail |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| School 1 | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| School 2 | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nLet $E_{ij}$ denote the expected frequency in the $i$th row and $j$th column. To calculate the expected frequencies, we multiply each probability by $N=60$, the total number of sample elements. For example,\n\n$$\nE_{11} = 60\\cdot 0.28 = 16.8\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our original table, we get the following:\n\n|  | Pass | Fail |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| School 1 | $O_{11}=18$<br>$E_{11} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16.8}$ | $O_{12}=6$<br>$E_{12} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7.2}$ |\n| School 2 | $O_{18}=24$<br>$E_{21} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}25.2}$ | $O_{12}=12$<br>$E_{22} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}10.8}$ |\n\nHere, $O_{ij}$ is the *observed* frequency in the $i$th row and $j$th column.\n\nNow that we've seen a concrete example, let's generalize a little." :tutorial/id #uuid "85f64329-0000-5f84-84b9-c52d3250fc18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10875 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173213 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\n\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\:\n\n\\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n\n- $n$ is the sample size\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/d0/d07c13f30dc4a0960b70f2705bb615f79c0c7ef3a6ed5d7a8eef2401db02d6c2.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\widehat{\\,p}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\widehat{\\,p}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\widehat{\\,p}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\widehat{\\,p}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\widehat{\\,p}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}, & \\,\\widehat{\\,p}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe719d03-f27c-50c1-8c5d-9bf9e9326e10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6255 :tutorial/title "Confidence Intervals for Proportions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173249 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our result for $S_{xy},$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{{\\beta }_{1}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, we compute the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as follows:\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}\\cdot 0+{\\beta }_{1}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the properties of $k_i$ discussed previously.\n\n- For the variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "719ed7ad-c96a-5299-b921-fda8c0b35e46" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7006 :tutorial/title "Expectation and Variance of Slope Parameter Estimates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173306 :tutorial/content "Consider a population where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Suppose we do *not* have an estimate for $p$, yet we want the smallest sample size $n$ required to ensure that the margin of error for a $95\\%$ confidence interval is at most $0.03$. Is this even possible without an estimate for $p$?\n\nAs we saw previously, the minimum sample size is given by\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}$, and consequently, we do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ either. However, to find a lower bound for $n$, we can simply replace the expression $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with its maximum value for $\\widehat{\\,p}\\in [0,1]$.\n\nA plot of the function $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ for different values of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given below.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ea9a3d120e0cd3907308a00182d86c94f1b8e23c61ec146e6b49c2ace93ea8.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ occurs when\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\\cdot \\left(1-\\dfrac12\\right) = \\dfrac14\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = \\dfrac14, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96, \\qquad \\varepsilon=0.03\n$$\n\ninto our inequality for $n$, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge (z_{α/2}^{2}\\widehat{p}(1 - \\widehat{p}))/(ε^{2}); = ((1.96)^{2})/((0.03)^{2}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}; \\approx 1067.11\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample size must be at least $n= 1068$.\n\nFinally, note that when $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is unknown, we often require a significantly larger sample size to ensure the same margin of error compared to the case where $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is known." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ccc7a5a-19cc-50ff-9f0f-96a8a25c112e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9471 :tutorial/title "Cases When an Estimate Is Not Given"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176954 :tutorial/content "Suppose a population is normally distributed with known mean $\\mu$ and *unknown* variance $\\sigma^2$. We can use empirical data to *estimate* the variance.\n\nFor example, let's assume our normal population has mean $\\mu = 12$ and unknown variance. Suppose an independent random sample is drawn from the population, and it is found that approximately $68\\%$ of the data in the sample have a value between $7$ and $17$.\n\nAccording to the empirical rule, we have the following:\n\n- Approximately $68\\%$ of the data falls within $1$ standard deviation of the mean.\n- Approximately $95\\%$ of the data falls within $2$ standard deviations of the mean.\n- Approximately $99.7\\%$ of the data falls within $3$ standard deviations of the mean.\n\nWe can assume that $100\\%$ of the data falls within $4$ (or more) standard deviations of the mean.\n\n![](images/53/530392e9b21a099bfc6a4f4cbee2ac4adba729cfbcfa3127ded2ebbe60e85a45.png)\n\nSo, according to this rule, $68\\%$ of the observations of a random normal variable $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ fall in the interval $(\\mu-\\sigma, \\mu+\\sigma)$. This range represents $2$ standard deviations. Therefore, we can write:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ - \\widehat{σ} &= 7 \\\\\nμ + \\widehat{σ} &= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have used $\\widehat{\\sigma}$ since the sample data will give an *estimate* of the population standard deviation $\\sigma$. Since the observations used to formulate these equations came from sample data, we cannot assume that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\sigma} = \\sigma\n$$\n\nHowever, if the sample is large, $\\widehat{\\sigma}$ should give a reasonable approximation to $\\sigma$.\n\nWe wish to solve for $\\widehat{\\sigma}$. First, we subtract the first equation from the second, which gives\n\n$$\n2\\widehat{\\sigma} = 17-7 = 10\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\sigma} = \\dfrac{10}{2} = 5\n$$\n\nFinally, the estimate of the population variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\sigma}^2 = 5^2 = 25\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ce69714-c709-5817-a219-438876ba2256" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10562 :tutorial/title "Estimators and Estimates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177047 :tutorial/content "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value.\n\nTo illustrate this concept graphically, consider the following example.\n\nThe owner of an ice cream shop keeps track of the average daily temperature $x$ (measured in $^\\circ \\mathrm{F}$) and the resulting daily ice cream sales $y$ (in dollars). The scatter plot for several randomly selected days and the corresponding linear regression line are shown below. \n\n![](images/fc/fc149b8d911a7a9863df9cc25c635d6155accd676e83e335a65d9083596b779b.png)\n\nSuppose the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny= 2.5 x +165.\n$$\n\nLet's use our regression line to compute the residual of the point with coordinates $\\color{red}(82, 360)$ (shown in red on our graph). \n\nFirst, notice that since the highlighted data point has coordinates ${\\color{red}(82, 360)},$ our actual value is $y = {\\color{red}360}.$\n\nNext, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{y} & =2.5x+165 \\\\  & =2.5(82)+165 \\\\  & =370\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/38/3843c71b50251ae035378507ffb22cfade48dff8e1544d8e1317e4e6d611f7a1.png)\n\nFinally, we compute the residual: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\widehat{y} \\\\  & =360-370 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThe residual tells us that the linear model *overestimates* the true volume of ice cream sales by $\\[math]10$ when the outside temperature is $82^\\circ\\,\\textrm{F}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7e5cd2f9-494e-5533-98e9-c24f42631dad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5968 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193893 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_0] = \\beta_0,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_0}] = \\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we define $k_i$ as\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} = \\overline{y} - \\widehat{\\beta_1}\\overline{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite our expression for $\\widehat{\\beta_0}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}} & =\\overset{―}{y}-{\\beta }_{1}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}}{{S}_{xx}}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to compute $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[{\\widehat{\\beta_0} }].$\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{n}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}]-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{E}[{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}[{\\beta }_{0}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}}}^{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\cancel{{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{x}_{i}}}^{1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i})-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}1+\\frac{{\\beta }_{1}}{n}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\beta }_{0}}{n}\\cdot n+\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1}-\\overset{―}{x}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the variance, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\widehat{{\\beta }_{0}}] & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}{\\sum }_{i}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}]+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}n{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}{\\sum }_{i}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sigma }^{2}+{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}{\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{S}_{xx}} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}(\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_0} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_0,\\sigma^2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9e3cbadc-9a0c-5925-af3f-b47ca4a2ff80" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6968 :tutorial/title "Expectation and Variance of Intercept Parameter Estimates"}]
